  "content": {
    "title": "How to watch NASA's Artemis II splashdown today",
    "text": "April 10, 2026, 4:08 a.m. CTNASA’s Artemis II crew is trading their space legs for sea legs as they complete a historic 10-day mission today.The four astronauts launched from Florida on April 1 and traveled around the moon with a close flyby of the far side—the first by humans in over 50 years. Now, nearing Earth, the crew prepares to splash down in the Pacific Ocean off California, ending their journey safely and spectacularly.The Artemis II crew: Commander Reid Wiseman, Pilot Victor Glover, Mission Specialists Christina Koch and Jeremy Hansen (Canadian Space Agency). Alabama residents can watch the Artemis II splashdown live. Here’s how to tune in today. What is Artemis II?Artemis II is NASA’s first crewed flight beyond Earth orbit since Apollo. Its goal: ensure the Orion spacecraft and SLS rocket are ready for longer lunar missions. Over 10 days, the crew orbited the moon, tested systems and collected data to improve future lunar missions.When and where will the Artemis II splashdown today?Friday, April 10, 2026, about 8:07 p.m. ET/5:07 p.m. PT/7:07 p.m. Alabama time.Pacific Ocean off the San Diego coastU.S. Navy teams will guide the capsule and assist the astronauts as they exit.How to watch the Artemis II landing today?NASA will stream live coverage of re-entry and splashdown on NASA+, YouTube and the NASA app.Coverage starts at 6:30 p.m. ET, 5:30 p.m. Alabama time.How the return worksOrion will re-enter Earth’s atmosphere at 24,000 mph—enough to cross New York to Tokyo in under 20 minutes. The capsule slows as it descends, deploying parachutes for a precise Pacific Ocean landing where recovery teams await.Engineers adjusted Orion’s return angle after heat shield damage during Artemis I. This steeper re-entry limits exposure to 5,000° F heat, says space.com.What does a NASA or SpaceX splashdown look like in the sky?It's pretty common for those on Florida's Space Coast (Cocoa Beach, Melbourne), the Fun Coast (Daytona Beach area) and the Treasure Coast (from Vero Beach to Fort Pierce) to watch a rocket launch or two every week.Millions watched Artemis II launch from NASA's Kennedy Space Center in Cape Canaveral.Will people in California be able to see Artemis II's Orion capsule splashdown off the coast of San Diego?Rocket launch photos are very common. It's a little harder to find photos of a NASA or SpaceX splashdown on social media. But depending on weather and cloud cover, it's possible. And sometimes, the space-related splashdown is seen far beyond where the capsule lands. Some examples include:When NASA's SpaceX Crew-7 mission to the International Space Station ended, the crew had a successful splashdown off Pensacola, Florida, in the early hours of March 12, 2024. A video on social media site X shows a streak of light in the sky, similar to a late-night rocket launch, visible all the way in Cleveland, Mississippi.On Nov. 8 2021, a SpaceX Dragon capsule cut across the night sky above parts of the U.S. and Mexico as the spacecraft made its way back to Earth for a splashdown in the Gulf of Mexico.On Sept. 4, 2023, NASA's SpaceX Crew-6 splashed down off the coast of Jacksonville, Florida, and the streak in the sky could be seen on the other side of the state, over Tampa Bay, according to the National Weather Service in Tampa Bay. People in Tampa reported seeing the SpaceX Dragon Endeavour light up the sky. Interestingly, the capsule left a streak over NASA's Kennedy Space Center in Cape Canaveral, Florida.Artemis II tracker: Follow moon mission locationWant to follow the astronauts along the Artemis II lunar journey? NASA has an Artemis II tracker available online and on its mobile app that allows users to see where Orion is, how fast it's traveling and how far the spacecraft is from both the Earth and the moon.The mobile version for smartphones even includes an augmented reality feature that allows users to move their phones to see where Orion is relative to Earth.The tracker, officially referred to as the \"Artemis Real-time Orbit Website\" (AROW), uses data collected in real time by sensors on the Orion spacecraft sent to the Mission Control Center at NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston. That information will be constantly updated beginning about one minute after liftoff until Orion begins its re-entry into Earth's atmosphere about 10 days later.Eric Lagatta is the Space Connect reporter for the USA TODAY Network. Reach him at elagatta@usatodayco.com.Jennifer Lindahl is a Breaking and Trending Reporter in Alabama for USA TODAY’s Deep South Connect Team. Connect with her on X @jenn_lindahl and email at jlindahl@usatodayco.com.",
